    "code": "M62.838",
    "rawCode": "M62838",
    "shortDesc": "Other muscle spasm",
    "longDesc": "Other muscle spasm",
    "plainEnglish": "A muscle spasm is when one or more muscles suddenly contract involuntarily and tighten up, causing a brief period of stiffness or cramping that you can't control. This code describes muscle spasms that don't fit into other more specific categories—they can happen in various parts of the body for reasons like overuse, strain, dehydration, electrolyte imbalances, or stress, and typically feel like a sudden tight knot in the muscle that may be painful or uncomfortable.",
    "whoUses": "Primary care physicians, orthopedic specialists, physical medicine and rehabilitation doctors, and neurologists use this code when diagnosing generalized muscle spasms that don't have a more specific underlying cause. It commonly appears on patient EOB statements for muscle-related complaints and is frequently used in outpatient settings, physical therapy, and rehabilitation contexts.",
